  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nA few morning showers impacted areas mainly east of Highway 281\nthis morning but have since pushed east with mostly sunny skies\nand warm temperatures encompassing the entire forecast area. An\nupper level disturbance in southwesterly flow aloft could impact\nportions of the local area late this evening and into the\novernight hours, and increased pops some for tonight to put in\nbetter agreement with the 18Z HRRR/other guidance. While severe\nweather is not likely, these high based storms will be capable \nof producing gusty winds, and the marginal risk for severe \nweather was expanded to include areas mainly west of Highway 283\ntonight. If realized, wind gusts of 50 to 60 mph will be \npossible with the strongest storms. Otherwise, these storms\nshould be on the weakening trend as they cross the local area\novernight, bringing mainly scattered light precipitation to\nparts of the local area.\n\nFor Sunday, another hot afternoon is in store for the local area\nas the ridge of upper level high pressure persists across the \ncentral/southern plains and southwesterly flow continues aloft.\nThis will once again result in afternoon heat index values \nclimbing to near - or slightly above 100 - especially across our\nKansas counties. At the same time, another round of nocturnal \nconvection will be possible, but the chance for severe or \nwidespread thunderstorms is not likely Sunday night. In fact, \nthis general weather pattern of above normal temperatures and \noccasional, mainly small chances for thunderstorms is \nanticipated to continue through much of next week with a return \nto more seasonal/cooler temperatures not hinted at in ensemble \nguidance until possibly the 2nd week of September.",
